 @extectic  It's more like they lock up features to force professionals to pay more. When I needed good double precision performance, I had to get a Tesla gpu. It meant paying 3 to 5 time more for the exact same chip, but I didn't have a choice because this feature is why I needed a GPU to start with.

Now, that doesn't mean that it's a "cash grab". It saves me computation time, and I pay for the extra cost of the ludicrous amount of memory on these cards, and for the ecosystem I need to even use them. Which involves hundreds of developpers paid by NVIDIA to develop pretty advanced stuff in CUDA and co. Pro stuff always comes with a premium because you pay for features and support which is much more demanding than what gamers need.

Now, to gamers who get 3090s and help pay for the devs needed to support other applications on it, thanks for subsidizing people like me !

