 @ruukinen  VR, not flat games.

 a quest 2 be headset you can go buy for £300 has a native render resolution of 5408x2736, vr always renders above what the panel is.
Of course until now no GPU could push that resolution above 90fps, so now the GPUs can do it, you also need the Vram for high quality textures at that resolution.

 @ruukinen just because the game shows its not maxing doesn't mean it won't use it, same way your system will game on 16gb of ram and show it's using well under until you check and see that windows is actually using cache, if it didn't do that it would straight up hard crash.

Running that high of a render resolution on vr on something like HLA or MOH, Asgard's wrath, racing Sims and flight sims with high or ultra textures, it will use more, I simply can't get my current GPU to display more that it's available Vram as that's how it works, doesn't mean it's not being held back.

And like mentioned already, just look at what unreal engine 5 is capable of and guess what it will need for that fancy megascan textures, you want future proof you need more than 12gb.

